Summary: Keep the cursor clip in step when the clipping window moves or resizes
Category: input
Kind: fix
Origin: wine-mac-builds
  Note: after Second Chance's workaround (it re-clips on NSWindowDidResize
    and NSWindowDidMove)
Upstream: candidate (draft in .local/bug-reports/)
Why: The Mac driver clips the cursor with -[NSWindow
 setMouseConfinementRect:] on the front Wine window. That rect is in the
 window's own coordinates and is cut to the window's frame when clipping
 starts, so once the window moves or grows it still holds the old area. A
 program asking for the same screen rect again changes nothing: the
 controller returns early when the rect is equal. Now a WineWindow tells
 the controller whenever its frame changes, including when Wine set the
 frame itself, and the confinement handler works the rect out again for
 the clipping window.
 With a debug build, a program that clips over its 400x300 window and then
 grows it to 1920x1080 kept the confinement {0,0,400,300} without the
 update, and got {10,0,1910,1080} with it.
Programs: Warnings at Waverly Academy, plain Wine with EmulateModeset on a
 loaded MacBook (Second Chance, 2026-11-13): the game sized its window to
 the mode (800x600 at the top left), switched mode, and win32u clipped to
 the window before the driver scaled the window up to the display, so the
 pointer stayed in the top-left 800x600 for good. On an idle Mac the window
 grew first.
Uncertain: Only the confinement handler (the default) does this; the event
 tap handler (UseConfinementCursorClipping=n) clips to the screen rect and
 doesn't depend on the window. If the window moves so the clip rect no
 longer overlaps it, confinement is removed (NSZeroRect) while Wine still
 thinks it's clipping, and a ClipCursor with the same rect won't restore it.
No test: the confinement rect isn't visible to a Windows program, and the
 pointer can only be checked with relative mouse movement (a synthetic
 absolute move isn't confined)
Status: 11.22: 2026-11-22: on a console with host14 (watched by the user):
 Waverly Academy, plain driver, Second Chance's re-clip off: the pointer
 reached the display's bottom right after the mode switch (1 session, on a
 mostly idle Mac, where the bug didn't show before either).

diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_cursorclipping.h b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_cursorclipping.h
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_cursorclipping.h
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_cursorclipping.h
@@ -41,6 +41,10 @@
      */
     - (BOOL) setCursorPosition:(CGPoint)pos;
 
+    /* If provided, called when a window's frame has changed, in case the
+     * clipping depends on it. */
+    - (void) windowFrameDidChange:(NSWindow*)window;
+
 @end
 
 
diff --git a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_cursorclipping.m b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_cursorclipping.m
--- a/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_cursorclipping.m
+++ b/dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_cursorclipping.m
@@ -485,6 +485,17 @@
         return TRUE;
     }
 
+    /* The confinement rect is in the window's coordinates and was cut to its
+     * frame, so it's out of date once the window moves or changes size. */
+    - (void) windowFrameDidChange:(NSWindow*)window
+    {
+        if (!clippingCursor || window.windowNumber != clippingWindowNumber)
+            return;
+
+        [window setMouseConfinementRect:[WineConfinementClipCursorHandler rectForScreenRect:cursorClipRect
+                                                                                  inWindow:window]];
+    }
+
     - (BOOL) stopClippingCursor
     {
         NSWindow *ownerWindow = [NSApp windowWithWindowNumber:clippingWindowNumber];
